CRISP NOTES: FOOD ADULTERATION
________________________________________
 Definition
Food adulteration =
Reduction in quality of food by:
•	Adding inferior/foreign substances 
•	Removing essential components 
________________________________________
 Types of Adulterants
1. Intentional
•	Done deliberately for profit 
•	Examples: 
o	Sand, stones, chalk powder 
o	Water, dyes 
•	⚠️ Harmful to health 
________________________________________
2. Incidental
•	Due to negligence or poor handling 
•	Examples: 
o	Pesticides 
o	Rodent droppings 
o	Larvae 
________________________________________
 Packaging Hazards
Improper packaging (plastics) may cause:
•	Toxic chemical release 
•	Harmful reactions with food 
•	Residues after heating 
👉 Use food-grade materials only
________________________________________
 Food Laws (PFA Act, 1954)
Purpose:
•	Maintain food quality & purity 
•	Protect consumers 
•	Prevent sale of adulterated food 
________________________________________
Food is adulterated if:
•	Quality is reduced 
•	Contains harmful/cheap substances 
•	Stored unhygienically 
•	Contains rotten/infected material 
•	Has excess colors/preservatives 
________________________________________
 Standards
AGMARK
•	Quality certification for agricultural products 
•	Ensures correct weight & quality 
BIS (ISI)
•	Sets standards for food products 
•	Certification ensures safety & quality 
________________________________________
 Food Contaminants
1. Biological
•	Bacteria, viruses, fungi 
•	Cause infections, diarrhea 
________________________________________
2. Chemical
•	Pesticides, heavy metals 
•	Cause cancer, organ damage 
________________________________________
3. Physical
•	Glass, metal, hair 
•	Cause injury 
________________________________________
4. Allergenic
•	Nuts, milk traces 
•	Cause allergic reactions 
________________________________________
 Common Contaminants (India)
•	Urea in milk 
•	Chalk in flour 
•	Detergent in paneer 
•	Artificial colors in sweets 
________________________________________
 Prevention
•	Wash fruits/vegetables 
•	Cook properly 
•	Avoid unhygienic food 
•	Check FSSAI label 
•	Store food properly 
________________________________________
 Food Additives
Definition:
Substances added to improve:
•	Taste 
•	Appearance 
•	Shelf life 
________________________________________
Types:
•	Preservatives (sodium benzoate) 
•	Colors (tartrazine) 
•	Sweeteners (aspartame) 
•	Antioxidants (Vitamin E) 
________________________________________
Benefits:
•	Longer shelf life 
•	Better taste & appearance 
•	Nutritional improvement 
________________________________________
Risks:
•	Allergies 
•	Hyperactivity 
•	Long-term health issues 
________________________________________
 Natural Toxins
Sources & Effects:
•	Potatoes → solanine → vomiting 
•	Cassava → cyanide → toxic 
•	Mold → aflatoxins → cancer 
•	Fish → toxins → paralysis 
________________________________________
Prevention:
•	Proper cooking 
•	Avoid spoiled food 
•	Proper storage 
________________________________________
 Food Fortification
Definition:
Adding nutrients to food for better health
________________________________________
Objectives:
•	Prevent deficiencies 
•	Improve public health 
________________________________________
Types:
•	Mass (salt + iodine) 
•	Targeted (school food) 
•	Market-driven (fortified cereals) 
________________________________________
Examples:
•	Salt → iodine 
•	Milk → Vitamin D 
•	Flour → iron 
________________________________________
Advantages:
•	Cost-effective 
•	No diet change needed 
•	Wide reach 
________________________________________
Limitations:
•	Overconsumption risk 
•	Not accessible to all 

